    {
      "id": "p0192-b007",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Normally, this would be written in Japanese as te o araimasho, which means \"let's wash our hands\". But in Japanese entertainment, titles that end in sho are sometimes replaced with the English word \"show\". This wordplay adds an extra layer of flair to the title in question, so this practice is particularly common with flashy entertainment. And, of course, Mettaton is all about being flashy.",
        "raw": "Normally, this would be written in Japanese as te o araimasho, which means\n\"let's wash our hands\". But in Japanese entertainment, titles that end in sho are\nsometimes replaced with the English word \"show\". This wordplay adds an extra\nlayer of flair to the title in question, so this practice is particularly common with\nflashy entertainment. And, of course, Mettaton is all about being flashy.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
